REBASE_HEAD is not an unresolved-rebase sentinel: it survives a successful conflicted rebase, so the gate refuses to run for exactly the lane that just resolved a conflict, and the escape hatch it names does not work through just rtest

FOUND by lane-tooling while re-gating B162, blocking the merge until worked
around. Measured from scratch, not inferred.

THE REFUSAL. `just rtest` refused with "the working tree has an unresolved
rebase" on a tree where the rebase had reported success and `git status` was
clean. Three independent checks say there is no unresolved operation:
  - no `rebase-merge/` or `rebase-apply/` in the worktree gitdir, the common dir,
    or ANY worktree in the repo
  - `git status` clean, 0 unmerged paths
  - git's own verdict: `git rebase --continue` -> `fatal: no rebase in progress`

What IS present is `REBASE_HEAD`, which `GIT_OPERATION_SENTINELS` treats as an
unresolved-rebase sentinel.

REPRODUCED IN A THROWAWAY REPO:

                        rebase-merge/    REBASE_HEAD
  mid-conflict          present          present
  after SUCCESSFUL
    conflicted rebase   absent           STILL PRESENT
  after CLEAN rebase    absent           absent

So `REBASE_HEAD` marks "a rebase happened and had conflicts", not "a rebase is
unresolved". It survives completion. The clean-rebase row explains why this never
fired across ~8 clean rebases tonight and fired on the first conflicted one.

IT IS THE ODD ONE OUT, AND THAT IS THE ROOT CAUSE. `MERGE_HEAD` is present
mid-conflict and REMOVED BY GIT ON RESOLUTION. The sentinel list conflates "git
writes this during the operation" with "git writes this only while unresolved",
and those two differ for exactly one of the four HEAD-style entries.

THE SELFTEST PINS THE WRONG PREMISE RATHER THAN MISSING IT.
`remote-build.py:864` hardcodes `("REBASE_HEAD", "rebase")` DELIBERATELY
independent of the table, with a comment saying the hardcoding exists so an
omission cannot hide. Same shape as B106's selftest asserting exit 0 for the
input B162 turned out to be about: the guard is well-built around a false
premise, so its own test defends the defect. Fixing the table without fixing
that assertion will fail, and fixing the assertion without understanding why it
was hardcoded will lose the omission protection it was written for.

THE REMEDIATION ADVICE IS UNUSABLE AS WRITTEN -- measured, not assumed. The
message says "Pass --allow-unresolved". Through `just rtest`, the command every
lane is told to run, flags land after the action and never reach the flag parser,
so `just rtest --allow-unresolved` reprints THE IDENTICAL REFUSAL. A reader
following the advice literally loops. Same class as B162's unreachable-repair
trap, in the same file, found the same night. The working form is
`python3 scripts/remote-build.py --allow-unresolved test`.

IMPACT. It fires on precisely the lane that resolved a conflict and is
re-gating -- the workflow this batch mandates -- and the escape hatch it names
does not work through the documented entry point. Four lanes hit conflicted
rebases tonight.

THE WORK:
  1. Remove `REBASE_HEAD` from the sentinel set; detect an unresolved rebase by
     `rebase-merge/` or `rebase-apply/`, which is what git itself uses.
  2. Fix the `remote-build.py:864` assertion, and preserve the omission
     protection it was written for by some means that does not require the
     premise to be true.
  3. Make the remediation advice reachable from `just rtest`, or say the
     `python3 scripts/remote-build.py ...` form explicitly in the message. Do not
     print advice that cannot be followed through the documented entry point.
  4. Audit the other three HEAD-style sentinels against the same
     during-vs-unresolved distinction. `MERGE_HEAD` is known correct; the others
     have not been checked and share this one's provenance.

REQUIRED EVIDENCE: RED is the throwaway-repo table above, re-run. GREEN is a
successful conflicted rebase followed by `just rtest` proceeding. POSITIVE
CONTROL is a genuinely mid-conflict tree still being refused -- without it the
fix could be "never refuse", and the red/green would look identical.

NOTE: `REBASE_HEAD` was deliberately NOT deleted from the affected worktree, so
the defect stays reproducible. Do not "fix" a lane by deleting it.

Related: B162 (the unreachable-repair trap), B168 (a guard well-built around a
false premise, whose own test defends it).